<dict>
	<key>approval</key>
	<dict>
		<key>approvedOn</key>
		<string>2026-08-27</string>
		<key>decisionEffect</key>
		<string>The user approved this exact product identity tuple for downstream Phase 04.1 consumers. This approval is not legal clearance, permission to use provider marks, service authorization, or public-launch authorization.</string>
		<key>decisionText</key>
		<string>approve exact tuple</string>
		<key>proposedCandidate</key>
		<string>Canis97</string>
		<key>proposalRationale</key>
		<string>Canis97 is a compact visual wordmark and a 1997 desktop-audio-era allusion. “Canis” is a deliberate, indirect reference to Sirius in Canis Major; “97” references Winamp's 1997 initial release without copying its name, trade dress, or mascot. It remains subject to attorney review before public launch or registration.</string>
		<key>state</key>
		<string>approved-product-decision</string>
	</dict>
	<key>attorneyReviewRequired</key>
	<true/>
	<key>candidateBrief</key>
	<dict>
		<key>excludedTermsAndImagery</key>
		<array>
			<string>Sirius</string>
			<string>XM</string>
			<string>SiriusXM</string>
			<string>official-affiliation wording</string>
			<string>provider logos or dog imagery</string>
			<string>channel artwork</string>
			<string>Apple hardware</string>
			<string>standard-control facsimiles</string>
			<string>other players' trade dress</string>
		</array>
		<key>goods</key>
		<string>Native macOS music and radio player for subscribers</string>
		<key>style</key>
		<string>Late-1990s/early-2000s desktop music-player culture: burned CDs, MP3 bitrates, bitmap skins, spectrum scopes, equalizers, and chunky desktop hardware. Prefer one or two easy syllables and avoid forced portmanteaux.</string>
	</dict>
	<key>candidateScreeningSources</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>candidate</key>
			<string>Cueamp / CueAmp / cueamp / Cue Amp / Cue-Amp</string>
			<key>checkedOn</key>
			<string>2026-08-27</string>
			<key>finding</key>
			<string>An archived 1983 Allied Radio catalog names the CONEX CueAmp UM33 as a stereo eight-channel cue and monitor amplifier for radio and television stations. The exact spelling and directly adjacent audio/broadcasting goods create an avoidable identity conflict.</string>
			<key>url</key>
			<string>https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-Catalogs/Distributors/Allied-1983.pdf</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>candidate</key>
			<string>Cueamp / Cue Amp</string>
			<key>checkedOn</key>
			<string>2026-08-27</string>
			<key>finding</key>
			<string>Dangerous Music uses “cue amp” as the ordinary name for a powered headphone/cue output. This is not an ownership record, but it confirms that the phrase is descriptive, category-common language in professional audio.</string>
			<key>url</key>
			<string>https://www.dangerousmusic.com/faq</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>candidate</key>
			<string>Bitjuke / BitJuke / Bit Juke / Bit-Juke</string>
			<key>checkedOn</key>
			<string>2026-08-27</string>
			<key>finding</key>
			<string>A public project description identifies BitJuke / BitTunes as a physical product for selecting songs from a mobile phone and paying via a Bitcoin wallet. This is a material music-device and user-selection collision for the proposed player.</string>
			<key>url</key>
			<string>https://www.linkedin.com/in/alinalebron</string>
		</dict>
